WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:02.267
-I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:04.067
You just came back from a trip
I want to ask you about.

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:06.667
You went to Finland?
-I did. I did.

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.200
Went with friends, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.300
Why did you -- Why Finland?
Any connection?

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.567
-Um, so, okay -- So,
my boyfriend, Joe Tippett,

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:14.934
has an affinity for

00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:16.701
photographing
the northern lights.

00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:19.534
So this was, like,
the pilgrimage to see

00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:21.801
the northern lights,
'cause he'd gone before.

00:00:21.801 --> 00:00:23.200
So, we got a small group
of friends together,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.367
we decided to go for New Year's,

00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:28.100
and we had
the most unbelievable time.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.534
-Yeah. It was just amazing.
-It was like a fairy tale.

00:00:31.534 --> 00:00:33.901
It was --
Just couldn't believe my eyes.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:35.133
-Almost can't believe
this photo's real,

00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:36.234
but this is a photo --

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:38.267
-This isn't even really
a great photo.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:41.067
Joe would be disappointed in
my -- This is my photography.

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:44.234
-But that looks like --
almost like a fake set on, like,

00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:45.234
a Christmas --

00:00:45.234 --> 00:00:47.334
-That actually --
This is awkward.

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:49.100
That is a fake photo from a set.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.634
-Oh, yeah, I was gonna say,
'cause that doesn't look real.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:53.501
Okay, good. But the real --
-If we could have them send --

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:55.534
-The real -- Yeah, send
the real photo eventually.

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:59.033
-Yeah.
-But, that -- So, you...

00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:00.200
You see that, right?

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.267
And what do you do?

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:02.467
I mean, what --
How do you react?

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.267
And what is it?
What is it, the northern lights?

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:05.868
-Oh...science.

00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:07.067
-I know. Yeah. I'm sorry to --

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:10.067
-No, it actually -- It's like --

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:17.000
It's particles of the atmosphere
interacting with space.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:21.000
-Now, is that real
or you just made that up?

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.767
-I think you can decide
for yourself.

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:25.834
-But you do that, and I go --

00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:27.434
-I looked it up,
too, when I was there.

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:29.000
I mean, they have all
these incredible activities,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.934
so I was more --
I was more, like, focused on

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:35.000
riding behind a reindeer,
and they have, like --

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.367
You can jump into the water.

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.200
-I wanted to ask you about this,
because they do --

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.501
They have a thing where you can
jump into a frozen lake.

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:42.567
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:44.400
And you posted
a little video of it,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.701
but tell me about
the experience.

00:01:45.701 --> 00:01:48.467
Was it amazing?
-Well, it was awful.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:49.968
That's a -- I don't know --

00:01:49.968 --> 00:01:54.601
The people that do the polar
plunges -- this is not who I am.

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:55.767
This is what
I've learned about myself.

00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:57.567
-You're not
a polar plunger at all, no.

00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:00.200
-No, I don't think so.
But I said yes.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.100
I was, again, trying to sort of,
like, impress my boyfriend.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.601
Apparently, I'm very insecure
about my boyfriend.

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:08.467
-No, this is --
He takes great photos, and...

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:09.667
But wait. So, what happens?

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:10.767
And what time
of the day is this?

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:11.901
-So, we went at night.

00:02:11.901 --> 00:02:14.167
We had this
beautiful spa that's, like,

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:17.267
right on the corner of the
little edge of the lake there.

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.567
And the guide
sort of brings you over there,

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.934
and they leave you
in this hot sauna.

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:24.767
And then they're like,
"The hole is over there."

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:27.033
And --
-"The hole is over there"?

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:28.634
That's not what they say.

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:30.767
-I'm not exagger-- Well --
-That's not what they say.

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.067
-There's a language barrier.
-Okay, yeah, sure, yeah.

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:34.334
So, you're in the hot tub.
You're getting all --

00:02:34.334 --> 00:02:37.033
-It's essentially, like,
a cut-out of the lake,

00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:39.567
and there's a ladder
and, like, a flashlight.

00:02:39.567 --> 00:02:41.567
And nobody's there.
There's no lifeguard.

00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:43.601
There's no attendants
or anything.

00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:46.100
So you're like,
"This is a terrible idea."

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.634
But we did it,
and it was terrible.

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:52.634
-But you go in, don't you feel,
like, free or something

00:02:52.634 --> 00:02:54.934
or don't you feel like
you're a new person?

00:02:54.934 --> 00:02:56.334
-No.
-Doesn't it feel like --

00:02:56.334 --> 00:03:00.601
-No. I felt cold and I wanted to
go inside and drink a beer.

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:02.767
-We have the video of you
doing the plunge in Finland.

00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:04.267
Let's take a look
at you in action.

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:06.434
Sara Bareilles.
-Great.

00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:07.868
Oh, my God!

00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:12.400
-Dude, you went for it.
-Aah! Oh, my God.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.767
Oh, that's awful!

00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:16.267
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:18.400
-You don't know where
you're going to go with that.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.434
You're like, "Oh, my God...
that's awful."

00:03:20.434 --> 00:03:22.167
-No, it's just a sinus headache.

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:25.567
It's an immediate, like,
headache, and I ran inside.

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:27.000
And then people were like,
"Let's go again!"

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.601
And I was like,
"Not on your life!

00:03:29.601 --> 00:03:30.834
No."

00:03:30.834 --> 00:03:34.634
-It is Pride Month,
and you wrote and recorded

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.934
your hit song "Brave"
almost 10 years ago.

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:41.667
It's become sort of
an anthem for -- Yeah.

00:03:41.667 --> 00:03:43.968
What does it feel like
when you --

00:03:43.968 --> 00:03:45.801
When people play that
or come up to you and go,

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:47.367
"Thank you for writing
that song" or...

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:49.734
-Oh, my gosh, it is --
it's, like,

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:52.334
the dream, as a songwriter,
to have a song kind of take on

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:53.667
a life of its own.

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:57.968
And I wrote it as a love letter
to a dear friend of mine

00:03:57.968 --> 00:03:59.334
who was struggling with
coming out,

00:03:59.334 --> 00:04:03.467
and then, over the years,
the song has kind of taken on

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:06.701
a few different lives of its own
and been embraced by

00:04:06.701 --> 00:04:11.501
all kinds of communities and
especially the LGBTQ+ community.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.767
And I have so many stories
and letters from people

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:15.901
who said that it was a part of
their coming-out story.

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:18.601
And I just think
it's just so beautiful.

00:04:18.601 --> 00:04:19.834
It's so meaningful to me.

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:22.067
-Congrats on that.
I mean, you're so talented.

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:25.200
And I love that
you get to do that

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.300
and then you get to do
"Girls5eva."

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.267
-Me too.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:32.567
That is, like --

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:34.601
Two seasons are streaming
right now on Peacock,

00:04:34.601 --> 00:04:35.634
I should mention.

00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:37.234
-None of your friends
are on the show.

00:04:37.234 --> 00:04:40.067
-I don't know anyone
involved with this thing.

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:42.434
But when Tina Fey
calls you and says like,

00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:43.567
"Sara, we should
do this thing" --

00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:45.133
Did you guys
know each other before or...

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.501
-She had -- I mean,
I knew her a little bit.

00:04:47.501 --> 00:04:50.501
Mm, I mean, I had, like,
stood next to her in line

00:04:50.501 --> 00:04:52.567
at a bathroom at a charity.

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:55.934
-That's a great story.
-I think she'll remember.

00:04:55.934 --> 00:04:57.300
And I didn't have, like,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.868
the cojones
to say anything at that point.

00:04:59.868 --> 00:05:02.801
-But I was like, "Oh, my God,
I'm next to Tina Fey."

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:05.133
And then she had came to see
me in "Waitress,"

00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:07.100
and so she had seen me perform.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:10.567
And, yeah, then I got the call
that they were interested

00:05:10.567 --> 00:05:13.234
and had my first Zoom
with Tina and Meredith and --

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:14.567
-Oh, Meredith is amazing.

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:17.267
-Oh, my God, Meredith Scardino,
our creator, showrunner.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:18.367
-Yep.

00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:21.968
-And I was not graceful or cool

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:23.834
or sophisticated at all
in my meeting.

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:24.868
-No? What were you like?

00:05:24.868 --> 00:05:27.200
-I, like, screamed,
"I'm so nervous!"

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.067
-[ Laughing ] Oh, my gosh.

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:31.300
On the Zoom.
And they're like, "Oh."

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.300
And I'm like,
"Are you sure that you want me?

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.534
I don't --
I've never done this before.

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:36.033
I'm not sure I can do it."

00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:38.434
Which I've learned
is not the best thing to say

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:40.567
in your first...

00:05:40.567 --> 00:05:42.334
-How did you
psyche yourself out of that?

00:05:42.334 --> 00:05:43.767
How'd you say
that you can do it?

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:45.901
-Well, I actually had
a sweet conversation --

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:50.033
Again, this is a conversation
about my boyfriend.

00:05:50.033 --> 00:05:51.701
-Sounds like a great guy.
We all approve.

00:05:51.701 --> 00:05:54.334
We love that guy.
Well-traveled.

00:05:54.334 --> 00:05:57.534
Got an eye for the stars,
you know?

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:59.501
-Yeah. Yeah, he's a good guy.
-Yeah.

00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:03.701
-He kind of reminded me --
I was struggling with

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:05.267
insecurities about
being able to sort of meet

00:06:05.267 --> 00:06:07.901
the challenge of it, and I felt
really out of my depth.

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:10.901
And he's like, "If Tina Fey
believes that you can do this,

00:06:10.901 --> 00:06:12.234
why don't you just trust her?

00:06:12.234 --> 00:06:14.033
Like, if you can't
find it in yourself,

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:15.834
just trust that
one of your heroes

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:18.334
is not going to put you
in position to fail."

00:06:18.334 --> 00:06:20.701
And that was really good advice.
So...yeah.

00:06:20.701 --> 00:06:22.434
-Hey, I love that.
See, that's --

00:06:22.434 --> 00:06:24.634
-Joe, come on out.
-No, he's not here.

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:26.434
No, Joe's not here.

00:06:26.434 --> 00:06:28.000
"Come on out."

00:06:29.267 --> 00:06:31.467
I would love if I surprised you
with Joe somewhere coming out.

00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:33.400
-I'm like,
"I'll see you at home, later."

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.067
-Yeah, "Why are you
coming out here?"

00:06:35.067 --> 00:06:36.667
Let's talk "Into the Woods,"
can we please?

00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:37.901
-Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:43.200
-This is
Stephen Sondheim -- legend.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.901
I mean, one in a zillion.

00:06:45.901 --> 00:06:49.400
This is -- When was the last
time this has been on Broadway?

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:53.434
-So, I think the story
sort of goes --

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:57.634
So, this is the first production
that is going into production

00:06:57.634 --> 00:06:59.267
since Stephen Sondheim's
passing,

00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:00.634
so it's been
a really tender time

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:01.901
for the creative team.

00:07:01.901 --> 00:07:04.968
And Steve and James Lapine,
who wrote the book,

00:07:04.968 --> 00:07:06.834
are always very involved with
their productions.

00:07:06.834 --> 00:07:10.734
And, so, it was written
during the '80s,

00:07:10.734 --> 00:07:12.501
sort of surrounding
the AIDS crisis,

00:07:12.501 --> 00:07:15.634
and then it was
remounted again after 9/11.

00:07:15.634 --> 00:07:17.834
And now this
seems to be the next --

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.634
There was another production,
but this is, I think,

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:22.200
the next production on Broadway.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:26.467
-And do you feel pressure
when you do such a --

00:07:26.467 --> 00:07:27.767
like, a work
of Stephen Sondheim

00:07:27.767 --> 00:07:29.868
or do you go like,
"Well, you know what?

00:07:29.868 --> 00:07:31.400
I love you and you're not here,

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.501
and this is a way
to say I love you"?

00:07:33.501 --> 00:07:36.734
-I do just try to surrender

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:40.734
and honor the material
as best as I can.

00:07:40.734 --> 00:07:44.200
We did a two-week run of this
at Encores! at City Center,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:48.000
and the process is so quick,
you almost don't have time to

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.300
think about anything.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.667
So, we're again kind of doing
this really quickly.

00:07:51.667 --> 00:07:52.968
We open at the end of
the month, you know?

00:07:52.968 --> 00:07:55.567
So it's fast.

00:07:55.567 --> 00:07:57.033
But I think you have to trust --

00:07:57.033 --> 00:08:01.300
This show is so special and
so beloved for so many reasons.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.868
It's really dense and complex
and deals with

00:08:04.868 --> 00:08:06.467
really deep subject matter

00:08:06.467 --> 00:08:08.667
with a lot of humor
and a lot of heart.

00:08:08.667 --> 00:08:11.367
And the songs are amazing.
This cast is bonkers.

00:08:11.367 --> 00:08:15.801
-Yeah.
Will Joe make an appearance?

00:08:15.801 --> 00:08:18.434
-I'm sorry, who?

00:08:18.434 --> 00:08:20.400
-Joe.
-Joe.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.667
-You know Joe. Yeah.

00:08:22.667 --> 00:08:25.167
But Joe -- Joe may or may not be
there opening night.

00:08:25.167 --> 00:08:28.767
-He's going to be so pleased how
much he got mentioned in this.

00:08:28.767 --> 00:08:30.534
-He really did
get mentioned a lot.

00:08:30.534 --> 00:08:31.601
I can't wait to meet him.

00:08:31.601 --> 00:08:32.834
Sara Bareilles, everybody.

00:08:32.834 --> 00:08:36.300
"Into the Woods"
returns to Broadway June 28th.

